AbelianVarieties/Homomorphisms/IntHom.lean
------------------------------------------
[ ] IsCommAddMonObj A                                                (l.35)
      Abelian varieties are commutative.

[ ] functorial_ker_int                                               (l.70)
      A homomorphism f : A → B induces A[n] → B[n].
      Needs [n] ≫ f = f ≫ [n]. Only the half n • f = f ≫ [n] exists
      (`int_action` in Homomorphisms/TorsionFree.lean, l.38), and it cannot
      be used here since TorsionFree.lean imports IntHom.lean. It needs
      moving, probably into AbstractNonsense.
      The def is currently stated for any f : A ⟶ B. It needs
      [IsAddMonHom f] (f commutes with [n] and sends ζ[A] to ζ[B]), and B
      needs to be commutative.

[ ] IsAddMonHom_functorial_ker_int                                   (l.90)
      The induced map A[n] → B[n] is a group homomorphism.

[ ] isFinite_int_hom                                                 (l.98)
      [n] : A → A is a finite morphism when n ≠ 0.

[ ] step₂'                                                           (l.119)
      The n-torsion A[n] is an affine scheme when n ≠ 0.


AbelianVarieties/TateModule.lean
--------------------------------
[ ] int_hom_rational                                                 (l.31)
      On points, [n] is multiplication by n: n • g = g ≫ [n].

[ ] rational_torsion                                                 (l.34)
      Every K-point of A[n] is killed by n.
      Still missing: the inductive step and the final goal.

[ ] torsion_map_comp                                                 (l.156)
      The maps A[n](K) → A[m](K) compose correctly: going n → m → k
      equals going n → k directly (for k ∣ m ∣ n).
      Done: the arithmetic n / k = (m / k) * (n / m).
      Still missing: the scheme-level identity
        ker_int_hom (k ∣ n) = ker_int_hom (m ∣ n) ≫ ker_int_hom (k ∣ m)
      (probably pullback.hom_ext + int_hom_comp).

[ ] tate_module_representation                                       (l.236)
      A homomorphism f : A → B induces T_ p (A) → T_ p (B).
      Depends on functorial_ker_int (IntHom.lean).
